<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Palghar district</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Palghar is a district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on the coast and hills north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, created in 2014 from the northern part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thane">Thane</a> district. It has a large Adivasi population, especially <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warli">Warli</a> and Koli communities, and includes the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a>, former capital of the princely state of Jawhar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/palghar-district.jpg" alt="Palghar district" width="320" height="215" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Raman Patel · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:View_from_Kosbad_Hill,_Dahanu,_Thane_-_panoramio.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Palghar district lies in the Konkan division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and forms the northernmost part of the state's Konkan lowlands. It runs from Dahanu in the north to Naigaon, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-sea">Arabian Sea</a> and the slopes of the Sahyadri, and borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thane">Thane</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nashik">Nashik</a> districts, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valsad">Valsad</a> district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gujarat">Gujarat</a> and Dadra and Nagar Haveli. Its hilly east, the jangalapatti, takes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a>, Mokhada and Vikramgad, while the coastal bandarpatti includes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Vasai</a>, Palghar and Dahanu.</p><p>The main river is the Vaitarna, the largest of the Konkan rivers, 154 km long, which rises in the Trimbakeshwar hills and reaches the sea through a wide estuary off Arnala. The district receives about 3000 mm of rain a year, and dams such as the Tansa Dam send its water to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Mumbai</a>, an arrangement that is debated among scholars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Vasai</a> taluka shows the cultural mix left by the Portuguese, above all in its nine or so large churches, and forts such as Vasai Fort, Arnala Fort and Mahim Fort stand in the district; a stupa of the Buddha in the same taluka is another of its historic places. Until 1 August 2014 the talukas of the present district were part of Thane District. After a demand for division lasting almost 25 years, the Cabinet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> approved the new district on 13 June 2014, and Palghar became the 36th district of Maharashtra on 1 August that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The district headquarters is the town of Palghar, and the district comprises eight talukas: Palghar, Vada, Vikramgad, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawhar-al-siqilli">Jawhar</a>, Mokhada, Dahanu, Talasari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasai">Vasai</a>. At the 2011 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> these talukas had 2,990,116 people; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marathi">Marathi</a> was the first language of 61.96%, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheduled-tribes">Scheduled Tribes</a> made up 37.39% of the population. Satpati is the largest fishing port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, Dahanu is known for its chickoo, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>'s first atomic power plant stands at Tarapur.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Palghar district” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Jawhar merged into Bombay State after Indian independence and is now in <b>Palghar district</b>, Maharashtra.</blockquote><a href="/state/jawhar-state">Jawhar State</a></li></ul></section>
